Z||000001||Oreif||05-22-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_Freak:<BR><B>    Anyone have a good idea of where all the chassis grounds are?  Thanks<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Which ones? The main engine one is hooked to the right side decklid hinge. (This is the one that always breaks) The other one is bottom left by the trans. The ECM is inside driverside firewall. Dash and interior is behind the radio.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero_Freak||05-22-2001||09:09 PM||||I'm thinking my problem is related to a ground.  Getting a code 32 and 34, but only when a/c is on and driven a little.  The voltmeter is low on startup until I rev a little and then it's fine.  I kick the a/c on and it goes way down.  I can turn on everything and the vent w/o a/c and it's not nearly as low?  Maybe a ground?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||05-22-2001||09:23 PM||||Sounds to me like you have a few different concerns.  First the voltage dropping way down with a/c on at idle. This is normal, with the a/c on your compressor clutch is now engaged and cooling fans have been turned on.  An idling vehicle with a/c on is about a 50-60 amp draw but an alt SI series alt like in a fiero can only produce around 30-40 amps at idle-at night you may also notice your headlights dimming at idle compared to just off idle.  <BR>So with that said I would still check to see if the alt belt is tight and if the volt gauge shows charging off idle you are probably all right.<BR>code 32 is EGR system failure. check archives for popular causes and failures.<BR>code 34 is low map sensor voltage, not to be confused with the 12v battery voltage but is its own voltage &lt;0.2v or 200mv using a 5v reference-but thats another story. again see if archives help with codes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||10:12 AM||||the one thing both those code have in common is vacuum  - check the vacuum tubes||||reg||1
